  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Quiet weather continues as temperatures warm up early\nthis week, next notable rain chance arrives Wednesday night \ninto Thursday.\n\nSeasonable temperatures accompany quiet and dry weather today across \nthe area, as high pressure remains embedded over the Mid-Atlantic. A \nweak upper-level shortwave will pass through the area tonight, which \ncould trigger a few light showers, but confidence is low in these \noccurring. Quiet weather then continues for the early part of the \nupcoming week, with no further rain chances until late Wednesday into\nThursday. \n\nHigh pressure pushes offshore into the Atlantic Monday into \nTuesday, with the clockwise flow around the high bringing \nwarmer temperatures back into the area. Highs will climb into \nthe 70s, with some Piedmont locations approaching 80 by \nWednesday ahead of the next system. That next system will move \nacross the Upper Midwest and into the Great Lakes Region by \nThursday, with the accompanying cold front bringing the best \nchance of rain to our area in the past several weeks. However, \nmodels have continued to back off on precipitation coverage, as \nthe system and upper-level trough both try to retreat farther \nnorth, causing less moisture and lift across our area. While the\nentire area is currently expected to still receive rainfall, \nthe best chances and higher totals will be along/west of the \nBlue Ridge.\n\nLingering light showers may stick around through the end of the \nweek, particularly for the western mountains due to upslope flow and \na another shortwave that passes through. Otherwise, quiet weather \nonce again returns through next weekend, with temperatures \nstill slightly above normal but cooling off around 5 degrees \nbehind the front.",
